<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Navajo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Diné, Navaho</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Navajo, who call themselves Diné, are an Athabaskan-speaking Native American people of the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herders</a> of sheep and skilled weavers and silversmiths, they raided and traded with the Spanish and Pueblo settlements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> for centuries. After the Long Walk to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosque-redondo">Bosque Redondo</a> in 1864, the Treaty of 1868 returned them to a reservation in their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>, now the Navajo Nation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/navajo.jpg" alt="Navajo" width="320" height="556" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuelito.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
